  "THE CLEANUP - a deletion-first dispatch on the whole ClarkHarness tree. CEO instruction 2026-08-22, verbatim: 'once the build is complete get a builder to cleanup our codebase; too much junk has accumulated which is biting us left right and center.'\n\nSEQUENCING, BINDING: runs AFTER the D11 fund-mode merge decision - D11 itself deletes the USE_FAKE_FIRESTORE tangle and the connector ternary, and a cleanup before it would conflict with the largest pending diff.\n\nMETHOD, TWO PHASES, NON-NEGOTIABLE ORDER: (1) INVENTORY FIRST - a deletion manifest where every item carries EVIDENCE it is dead: zero grep consumers, last git touch, and what would break if the evidence is wrong. The manifest is the reviewable artifact. (2) DELETE in small separable commits, full suite green after each batch, real exit codes.\n\nKNOWN CANDIDATES from the record (verify, do not trust): scratch_prop.json at repo root; stale root files (GEMINI.md, HANDOFF.md - check staleness); the seven standalone scripts that do not load .env; docs/README.md indexing NONE of the 17 docs filed 2026-08-21 (Donna, third consecutive day - fixing the index IS cleanup); dead code paths left after D11 (FUND_REAL_BROKER remnants, seeder refusal texts); test fixtures nothing imports.\n\nNEVER TOUCH: credential files (firebase_service_account*.json - flag, never delete), .env, Abhishek's surfaces (app/fund/thesis_generator/**, his types), docs/archives/** (append-only), any findings doc (never edited, never deleted), lean_workspace/algorithms/** (the quant's sandbox), anything on the CONTROL layer.\n\nThe builder's fitness question now scores DELETIONS - the firm runs 96:1 insertions-to-deletions and accretion finally has an owner. This is that mandate's first dedicated dispatch.",
