  "BLIND PASS ON THE EXCESS-RETURN CHALLENGE - the pass the validator's own recommendation demanded and the chair failed to route. CAUGHT BY THE CEO, 2026-08-22, verbatim: 'thsi says it needs to go to adversary before but its one of the top items in my desk?'\n\nTHE ARTIFACT: run-validator-breakeven recommendation 5, challenging the CEO's own 2026-08-21 excess-returns amendment. Its claim: the amendment assumes a belt return contains the risk-free rate; measured, belt returns contain ZERO rf on the cash portion (n=11 runs at exactly 0.000%), so converting end-to-end double-charges cash-parkers by up to 3.52%/yr. The seat states it does NOT ask to weaken the rule - it asks that the belt be made able to satisfy it honestly by crediting rf on idle cash inside the backtest first. BIL is already in fund_bars, 2,779 bars. No threshold value proposed.\n\nWHY THIS NEEDS YOU: THE SEAT ITSELF DECLARED THE DIRECTION LOOSENING. Constitution clause 5 - a challenge that would loosen a control, widen an envelope or remove a check goes to the adversary blind BEFORE it reaches the CEO, because a governance channel for revisiting decisions is exactly the shape a quiet loosening would arrive in.\n\nATTACK HARDEST AT THE PREMISE THAT LETS IT THROUGH: that this is INSTRUMENT REPAIR rather than a weakening wearing repair's clothes. The excess-returns amendment exists because the alternative was DEMONSTRATED to certify a zero-skill cash-heavy mix as premia - under rf=0 with free leverage, T-bill carry impersonates edge. If crediting rf on idle cash inside the backtest re-opens any part of that hole, this is a loosening and must be killed. Verify the 0.000% (n=11) and the 3.52%/yr independently.\n\nSEQUENCING, BINDING: do NOT run while the validator is in flight. It authored this artifact and is running a separate cost-model audit. Working protocol 3 is not negotiable for throughput.",
