  "PERSIST THE FILINGS CORPUS IN POSTGRES - INCREMENTAL FROM DAY ONE. CEO instructions 2026-08-22: \"lets make sure we capture data in postgres so we need to continue re-deriving and spend time on re-compute\" and \"once data size grows it will need a pipeline\". THE LOSS WE ARE ONE SESSION-END AWAY FROM: a 91,795-filing EDGAR pull sits in a scratchpad JSON (17.7 MB) that dies with the session. The analyst wrote \"the expensive pull, do not repeat it\" into its own memory - a note that will outlive the file it describes. Same for the cached bars and every measurement script from today. WHAT THE CORPUS ALREADY CONTAINS AND NOBODY HAS OPENED: form 4 x 49,409 (insider transactions), form 144 x 6,178 (NOTICE OF INTENT TO SELL restricted stock - forward-looking and dated), 8-K x 12,166, SC 13G/A x 2,940, form 3 x 2,242, DEF 14A x 1,102. The mechanism reported that only 5 of 19 menu entries scale in count and four are blocked on data. Insider transactions are count-scaling and IDIOSYNCRATIC BY CONSTRUCTION - not a factor bet - and we have fifty thousand of them sitting in a temp file. THE REASON IS NOT COMPUTE, AND THIS CHANGES WHAT TO STORE. Compute is free - measured today at 24 threads on 11 percent with an idle 4090. What is expensive is EDGAR rate limiting and wall clock, and more importantly REPRODUCIBILITY: a study you cannot re-run against the same input is an anecdote, not a measurement. The mechanism breadth numbers are only checkable if the bars they used still exist. WHAT TO CACHE AND WHAT MUST NEVER BE CACHED: cache EDGAR filings - immutable, dated, never restated - plus filing text and extracted fields and the event panel. DO NOT CACHE PRICE BARS: our closes are split-back-adjusted from TODAY, so caching freezes an anchor and the cached value silently stops matching the live one at the next split. THAT IS EXACTLY THE +43.84 pct/yr PHANTOM FACTOR WE FOUND TODAY, and caching would have made it permanent instead of discoverable. Do not cache derived statistics either - correlations, breakevens, capacity - they go stale and become the next phantom. PROVENANCE IS MANDATORY OR WE BUILD THE TRAP AGAIN: every row carries when it was pulled and what was known then. The fund already has app/fund/asof.py built for exactly this and barely uses it. Without it the corpus becomes another point-in-time landmine - queried in six months, answers with today, and nobody can tell. INCREMENTAL FROM DAY ONE, and this is the one thing that is painful to retrofit: do NOT build a re-pull-everything loader. EDGAR gives the key for free - accession numbers are unique and daily index files exist - so incremental ingest is nearly free if designed in and a rewrite if not. DO NOT build partitioning, workers or scheduling yet; that is anticipating a problem we have not measured, and the CEO own rule is that scale-anticipation is a step-2 problem. WRITE PATH: seats cannot write to Postgres and that boundary stays. The spine should ingest on a route rather than the chair loading it, because a chair-loaded corpus stops being loaded the day a chair forgets.",
