  "THE WIRE — make the desk's ROUTING a thing the code evaluates, not a sentence seats write in prose. CEO instruction 2026-08-22, verbatim: \"yes, but what I am saying that we need to get our wire right.\"\n\nSUPERSEDES dd22837c, which specified only the loosening gate. The CEO's point is that the gate was a symptom.\n\n## THREE FAILURES IN ONE DAY, ALL THE SAME DEFECT\n\n1. **A LOOSENING ITEM REACHED THE CEO UNGATED.** run-validator-breakeven rec 5 says in its own body \"THIS WOULD LOOSEN, so it goes to the adversary blind BEFORE the CEO\" — and then rendered as a top item on his desk. Constitution clause 5 is enforced by nothing. THE CEO FOUND THIS HIMSELF.\n\n2. **AN UPDATE CONSUMED A DECISION SLOT.** run-analyst-cycle4 rec 1 was filed `awaits-ceo` and asks for no click. CEO, verbatim: \"this seems like an update more than actionable... like I loved reading it since it was simple and positioned towards my core objectives.\" The writing was right; the CHANNEL was wrong.\n\n3. **A KILLED NUMBER SAT IN THE RECORD AS FACT.** The same row quoted +2.72%/yr and t_NW 2.66. The adversary later killed both (the screen sells at the close of the filing day; 86.8% of those filings are not public until after that close) and its first recommendation is \"do not record +2.72%/yr or t_NW=2.66 anywhere in the fund's record.\" The chair recorded a seat's unverified headline as though it were established.\n\n**THE COMMON CAUSE: a recommendation has exactly one channel to exactly one reader, and it carries no machine-readable statement of what it IS or what still has to happen to it.** Everything the routing needs lives in prose that nothing parses.\n\n## WHAT TO BUILD\n\n### (A) Four declared fields on every recommendation\n\n- `direction`: TIGHTENS | NEUTRAL | LOOSENS | UNDECLARED\n- `channel`: DECISION (needs a click) | UPDATE (needs a read) | EXECUTION (the chair does it, nobody clicks)\n- `next_actor`: ceo | chair | <seat> — three of these ALREADY EXIST, are validated, and NOTHING WRITES THEM (see a87f5b9b). Make them required, not optional.\n- `gates`: a list of preconditions, each either satisfied-with-a-citation or open. Start with two kinds: `adversary_pass` and `chair_verification`.\n\nSeats declare these. They are not inferred from prose — inference over free text is exactly what failed here, and the `kind` field already demonstrated the failure mode at 84 distinct values over 219 rows.\n\n### (B) The routing rules, enforced in code\n\n- `direction: LOOSENS` ⇒ **THE ITEM ROUTES TO THE ADVERSARY'S DESK AND NEVER APPEARS ON THE CEO'S.** CEO instruction 2026-08-22, verbatim: *\"It should go to adversary's desk directly.\"* This is the correction he made to an earlier draft of this ticket, which had the item STAYING on his desk marked GATED. He is right and the difference is the whole design: **`next_actor` is the routing key, and an item lives on exactly ONE desk — whoever's move it is.** The desk already claims that principle for ordinary rows and simply fails to apply it to gated ones. A LOOSENS recommendation's next actor is the adversary, so it is the adversary's row until a recorded adversary run cites it; only then does its next actor become the CEO and only then does it land on his desk.\n\n  Two consequences, both required:\n  - **Filing a LOOSENS recommendation CREATES the adversary's work item.** No chair has to remember to route it. Today the chair did not, and the CEO found the item on his own desk.\n  - **The chair's dependency check still binds and may DELAY the dispatch.** Working protocol 3 forbids the adversary running alongside the artifact's author; that sequencing is the chair's to perform and the desk must render \"queued behind its author\" as a real state rather than as silence. This is not hypothetical: it happened today, and it is the first time on record that the dependency check has said no.\n- `channel: UPDATE` ⇒ **never counts toward the CEO's decision load.** Renders in a briefing lane he can read and dismiss. It still renders — he said he WANTS to read these — it just stops impersonating a decision.\n- `channel: EXECUTION` ⇒ counts toward the CHAIR's load, not the CEO's. The CFO measured 115 items naming the chair against 12 naming the CEO; those are different queues and the desk currently blends them.\n- `gates` with anything open ⇒ not actionable by anyone, and the desk says WHICH gate and WHO clears it.\n\n### (C) FAIL CLOSED AND FAIL LOUD\n\n`direction: UNDECLARED` must NOT be treated as NEUTRAL. **Absence is never zero** — the fund's oldest non-negotiable. Render UNDECLARED as its own state and put the count somewhere a human sees it. Same for a missing `channel`.\n\n### (D) A PROVISIONAL state for unverified numbers\n\nA recommendation whose figures have not been chair-verified or adversary-reviewed renders PROVISIONAL, and its numbers render as claims-by-a-seat rather than as facts. This is the direct fix for failure 3. The chair should not be able to launder a seat's headline into the record by the act of recording it.\n\n## THE GENERAL FORM, and why this is worth building rather than remembering\n\n**This firm keeps writing governance rules as prose and discovering later that no code evaluates them.** The judgement register has the identical defect and it is already documented in the constitution: 17 of 19 entries carry a review trigger nothing evaluates, and the endpoint reports `triggers_unchecked: []` — absence rendered as zero, in the fund's own decision register. Clause 5 has now joined it.\n\nA rule nothing evaluates is a note. A register of notes reviews nothing. A wire made of prose routes nothing.\n\n## SCOPE AND TIER\n\nTouches the desk, the recommendation schema and the CEO page. Does NOT touch the guard, autopolicy, the gate, the risk engine, exit-rule mechanics or the event store. Build it; the chair reviews the diff as usual.\n\n**Sequencing note:** a87f5b9b already asks for the three next-actor fields to be WRITTEN by seats. That is part (A) of this ticket and should not be built twice — fold it in.\n\n**Do NOT auto-close anything as a side effect of this work.** Closing a dispatch remains an act of the chair's judgement (CEO instruction, 2026-08-21). This ticket routes items; it never decides them.",
